  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSurface high pressure will move off to the east tonight ahead of a \nweak mid level short wave that will move across the Ohio Valley \nSunday into Sunday night. We will be starting off quite dry across \nthe area, but an associated 20-30 knot low level jet ahead of this \nsystem will nose into southwest portions of our area heading into \nSunday evening. This will allow for things to begin to moisten up \nsomewhat later Sunday afternoon and into Sunday night. This will \nlikely be enough for some scattered shower development, with the best\nchances across our southwest, in the vicinity of the better forcing \nassociated with the low level jet. Suppose a few spotty showers could\nlinger into Monday, but generally expect a lull in any pcpn then \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nModels have come a bit more in line with the midweek system as the \nupper level trough axis over the central CONUS moves east into the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley region through Wednesday. This will bring\na developing chance for showers Tuesday night with fairly \nwidespread precipitation continuing into the day on Wednesday. Will \nthen linger some pcpn chances Thursday into Friday as some secondary \nmid level energy will try to move through the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley region.",
